.SH A) OVERVIEW
var creation (var-steps) are the creation of global or script specific
variables.  remember, global variables are everywhere, but can not carry
updates between units, just between scripts.

.SH B) TECHNICAL
var-steps are simply turned into c-language code as new variables.

.SH C) EXAMPLE
var-steps have four fields.  the leading spaces are changed to spacers (·)
in this manual for clarity.

   verb------- § desc § -func- § ---parms--------------------------------- §
   ···local    § sml  § - - -  § char  x = 15;                             §
   ···global   § lrg  § - - -  § char *p = "testing";                      §

.SH D) VERB
the verbs GLOBAL and LOCALare is expected to be indented three (3) characters
off-setting it from SCRP and later steps.  the verb field are is twelve (12)
characters, overflow and trailing spaces will be truncated.

GLOBAL can only be used in a PREP section and LOCAL can only appear right after
a SCRP, SHARED, GLOBAL, or CONFIG section header and before any COND.

   verb------- § desc § -func- § ---parms--------------------------------- §
   ···local    § ·    § ·      § ·                                         §
   ···global   § ·    § ·      § ·                                         §

.SH E) DESCRIPTION
it is very unhelpful to leave off a description as unit-testing is almost
invariably detailed, complicated, and extensive.  descriptions are a maximum
of thirty-five (35) characters, overflow and trailing spaces will be truncated.
legal characters are letters, numbers, and most punctuation.

since descriptions are demonstrated elsewhere, i have truncated them greatly
to fit everything in the available space ;)

   verb------- § desc § -func- § ---parms--------------------------------- §
   ···local    § sml  § ·      § ·                                         §
   ···global   § lrg  § ·      § ·                                         §

.SH F) FUNCTION
the third field is left blank.

   verb------- § desc § -func- § ---parms--------------------------------- §
   ···local    § ·    § - - -  § ·                                         §
   ···global   § ·    § - - -  § ·                                         §

.SH G) PARAMS
the fourth field is the actual variable definition code, same as if built
into the source c-program.

   verb------- § desc § -func- § ---parms--------------------------------- §
   ···local    § ·    § ·      § char  x = 15;                             §
   ···global   § ·    § ·      § char *p = "testing";                      §

.SH H) OUTPUT
when the unit-test is run, each var-step generates small block of output.
the output says it is the 1st step in the condition (aa) and is generated by
line 38985 in the source code.  everything else is a reflection of input values.
variables are never pass/fail in statistics.

  ···aa) LOCAL  : create working variable · ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·º38985»
  ·······local  :  29åtPROJ  *x_proj        = NULL;æ

NOTE: if the line number is [00555] is is in a specific unit test source file.
if like å00555æ it is in a shared file -- unit_data.unit or unit_wide.unit.
